Thanks, Claus. We do indeed spend a lot of time on the game (and, in some
cases, money -- the account I use for nethack development is paid for
out of my pocket).

> As for debts, I think that they do *owe* us a list of known serious
> bugs, posted here on a regular basis.  Instead I get complaints from
> them about how people keep emailing to them about "known" bugs.

Those bugs were all reported (and reported as being fixed in pl8) in
this newsgroup, some of them multiple times. People still e-mail the 
same reports to us. I think that tells us how many people pay attention
to bug reports on this group.

> [ Richard comments that we "owe" it to the users to put the ability
>   to recover from crashes in pl8 ]

It's much too late for that; besides which, if the game crashes, something
is *seriously* wrong, and restarting it is problematic at best.

As for the question of whether there should be a confirm for pools of
water/floating eyes/really big nasty monsters or whatever, it seems that
people support this for one of two reasons: (1) they have problems with
line noise or slow machine response, or (2) they're too lazy to look where
they're going. I don't know how the rest of the development team feels,
but frankly I think the users in category (2) get what they deserve.
The users in category (1) have a legitimate gripe, but the proper solution,
it seems to me, is to address the problem directly, e.g. to provide a
"flush" option to flush all characters in the input buffer before reading.
This would solve *all* the potential "I didn't really want to make that move"
problems that people have, without introducing a lot of extraneous
confirmations and without making floating eyes (for example) completely
useless.

All this will have to wait for 3.1. In the meantime, if you really don't like
something about the game, you've got the source code; go ahead and "fix"
it.
